# ohmail self-host configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        # then fill in the five required values below
#   docker compose up -d
#   docker compose logs api     # the first-run setup token is printed here, once
#
# Everything the stack reads is in this file. Values marked REQUIRED fail
# `docker compose config` with a message naming the variable; everything else has a
# working default baked into docker-compose.yml and only needs touching when the
# sentence beside it applies to your install.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ONE browser origin this install serves — scheme + host, nothing else.
#   https://mail.example.com   a real domain: Caddy provisions TLS automatically, provided
#                              the domain resolves to this box and ports 80/443 are reachable.
#   http://localhost           a same-box run, plain HTTP on loopback. Works in every major
#                              browser except Safari, which refuses the product's Secure
#                              cookies over http://localhost — use the HTTPS form there.
#   https://ohmail.test        a PRIVATE name on your own network (.test, .lan, .internal, or
#                              any name only your DNS knows). Set OHMAIL_TLS_INTERNAL=1 below
#                              — without it Caddy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate it can
#                              never issue, and retries for thirty days while your site serves
#                              no TLS at all.
# This value derives the cookie host and the passkey identity; changing it later signs
# everybody out.
OHMAIL_ORIGIN=

# Which of this box's addresses the front door listens on.
#   0.0.0.0        every interface — for a PUBLIC DOMAIN, where the certificate authority and
#                  everyone else must reach 80 and 443. Includes a VPS's public IP.
#   100.x.y.z      one private address — a tailnet (`tailscale ip -4`) or LAN interface.
#   127.0.0.1      loopback only, the default: nothing outside this box can connect.
#
# UPGRADING A PUBLIC INSTALL: earlier versions published on every interface with no way to say
# otherwise. Set 0.0.0.0 when you take the new file, or the stack comes back up on loopback and
# your domain stops answering. Check with
# `ss -tlnH | awk '{print $4}' | grep -E ':(80|443)$'`.
OHMAIL_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Set to 1 when OHMAIL_ORIGIN is a PRIVATE name — anything a public certificate authority
# cannot validate from the internet. Caddy then issues certificates from its own local CA
# instead of going to ACME. Leave it empty for a real public domain and for http:// origins.
#
# Browsers will not trust that CA until you install its root, which the stack holds here:
#   docker compose exec proxy cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt > ohmail-local-ca.crt
# Install that file as a trusted root on every machine that opens the app (on Arch:
# `sudo trust anchor ohmail-local-ca.crt`; on Debian/Ubuntu, copy to
# /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ and run `sudo update-ca-certificates`).
#
# Caddy picks its local CA automatically for `localhost`, so that origin needs nothing here. An IP
# address other than 127.0.0.1 cannot be OHMAIL_ORIGIN at all: passkeys need a name, and the server
# refuses to start on one.
#
# Setting this also turns OFF the HSTS header a public-domain install sends. A browser that
# has stored HSTS for a host refuses to let anyone click past a certificate warning on it,
# which on a locally issued certificate is a locked door rather than a warning.
OHMAIL_TLS_INTERNAL=

# Set to 1 when something else already terminates TLS in front of this stack — `tailscale serve`,
# a reverse proxy you run, a load balancer. The proxy then serves plain HTTP on port 80 and holds
# no certificate; OHMAIL_ORIGIN keeps naming the https address your terminator presents, which is
# what the cookie host, the passkey identity and every link the app writes come from.
#
# Point the terminator at port 80 on OHMAIL_BIND (`127.0.0.1:80` with the default). Port 443 has
# nothing behind it on this door. Leave empty to let this stack own TLS; the two are alternatives.
OHMAIL_EXTERNAL_TLS=

# Only read when OHMAIL_EXTERNAL_TLS is set: the addresses or CIDRs your terminator reaches the
# proxy from, separated by single spaces. The proxy believes `X-Forwarded-For` from there and
# nowhere else, and hands the api the one address it settled on. Leave it empty for a terminator on
# this same box: the proxy then trusts its network's gateway, where that terminator arrives from,
# read when the proxy starts and named in `docker compose logs proxy`. Set it when the terminator is
# on another host, or is itself a container on this stack's network; until then the api log names
# that terminator once as `forwarded_address_collapsed`, and every visitor through it shares one
# address. A value replaces the gateway rather than adding to it, so list the gateway too if a
# terminator on this box still forwards (`docker network inspect ohmail_default` shows it). A
# comma, other whitespace, an IPv4 address in IPv6 form (`::ffff:…`) or a word such as
# `private_ranges` could read differently to the proxy and the api, so the api refuses to start and
# says what to write. A value of nothing but whitespace counts as empty.
#
# The terminator must set or append `X-Forwarded-For` with the visitor's address: the proxy takes
# the rightmost address not in this list, the one the terminator added, as long as it is an address
# (an entry such as `unknown` is skipped, and the address to its left, which a visitor may have
# written, is taken). One that speaks HTTP adds one: Caddy on its own, nginx with
# `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;`. A relay that only passes the
# connection through (socat, stunnel, a TCP or layer-4 load balancer) adds nothing, and on this box
# it arrives from the same gateway, so a visitor can put any address in that header. With such a
# relay, or if you are not sure, set `0.0.0.0/32`: no connection comes from that address, so no forwarded
# address is believed and every visitor shares one. The cost: the password lock counts every
# visitor as one, so five wrong passwords from anyone lock that account for everyone until the lock
# ends.
#OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR=

# The database password (user and database are both `ohmail`, created on first start).
# URL-safe characters only — the value is embedded in a connection URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The key that encrypts mailbox credentials at rest. 64 hex characters:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Losing it means every connected mailbox has to be re-entered; treat it like a backup key.
# To ROTATE, add TF_KEK_V2 (then TF_KEK_V3) with a fresh value and KEEP the older versions
# set — new writes use the highest version, old rows still decrypt with theirs. Never replace
# TF_KEK_V1 in place: rows encrypted under it would become unreadable.
TF_KEK_V1=
#TF_KEK_V2=
#TF_KEK_V3=

# The credential for the bundled attachment-staging store (RustFS). Never leaves the
# compose network, but pick a real value:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
# Updating from the MinIO stack: this was MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD (and MINIO_ROOT_USER). The
# compose file still reads the old names when these are unset, so renaming them is optional.
RUSTFS_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# This install's ORGANIZER IDENTITY — the name its claim carries in each connected
# mailbox's `ohmail/_meta` folder, which is how every ohmail deployment (this one, the
# hosted service, a desktop install) agrees on WHO organizes a mailbox. Generate once:
#   echo "ohmail-selfhost:$(openssl rand -hex 6)"
# and never change it afterwards: a changed value makes this install read its own
# previous claims as a stranger's, and every mailbox then waits for you to press
# "Organize here" again. It must be UNIQUE to this install. Without a value the
# organizer falls back to the same identity the hosted service runs under, and two
# organizers sharing one name cannot tell each other's claims from their own — the
# exact dual-organizing the claim exists to prevent, reached the moment a mailbox has
# ever been organized by ohmail Cloud or by a second install.
TF_ORGANIZER_INSTALL_ID=

# What this server calls ITSELF on other people's screens. The identity above is an opaque
# handle; this is the string a person reads on their desktop app when it says who organizes
# their mailbox, and the one written into `ohmail/_meta` for any mail client to see.
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY unless you want something other than your own address there: empty means the
# organizer derives the name from OHMAIL_ORIGIN's host, so `https://mail.example.com` reads as
# "Organized by mail.example.com", which is true and is what an operator's own users expect.
# Set it only to say something the origin does not:
#   TF_ORGANIZER_DISPLAY_NAME=Front office server
TF_ORGANIZER_DISPLAY_NAME=

# ── Outbound mail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where product mail (address verification, new-device notices) is submitted. The default
# is the bundled mailpit SINK: mail is viewable at http://127.0.0.1:8025 on the box and
# delivered nowhere. Point this at a real relay to deliver for real:
#   SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465
# SMTP_URL and MAIL_FROM travel together — setting one without the other refuses the boot.
#SMTP_URL=smtp://mailpit:1025
#MAIL_FROM=ohmail@selfhost.example
#MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# The port the bundled sink's mail UI takes on the box's loopback. Change it only for a second
# stack on this box, whose sink would otherwise stop the stack from starting.
#OHMAIL_MAIL_UI_PORT=8025

# The compose project name — what this stack's containers, volumes and network are called. Leave
# it alone unless you run TWO stacks on one box: two stacks sharing this name are one project, and
# the second `up` adopts and mutates the first's containers. Give each its own value, directory,
# `.env`, OHMAIL_BIND and OHMAIL_MAIL_UI_PORT. Changing it on a running install orphans its volumes,
# database included.
#OHMAIL_PROJECT=ohmail

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ohmail images default to the release docker-compose.yml ships with. Set another released
# version to stay on it, e.g.
#   OHMAIL_IMAGE_TAG=0.9.8
#
# Not `latest`, which can skew: the three images are three registry tags and no registry moves
# three at once. A release writes every `<version>` tag before it moves `latest`, so a pull landing
# inside those last writes can fetch a new server beside an old organizer, which can accept mail
# scheduled for later without running its sender clock.
#OHMAIL_IMAGE_TAG=

# AI features (screening suggestions, reply drafting). Absent means those surfaces answer
# as unconfigured — everything else works. You choose the model and you pay for it.
#
# TF_AI_PROVIDER picks one of four, each with its own variable below. Left empty with
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set, it is Anthropic, as before:
#   TF_AI_PROVIDER=anthropic     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; requests go to api.anthropic.com
#   TF_AI_PROVIDER=openai        OHMAIL_OPENAI_API_KEY; requests go to api.openai.com
#   TF_AI_PROVIDER=opencode-go   OHMAIL_OPENCODE_GO_API_KEY and both models below; requests go
#                                to opencode.ai and on to the provider behind the model
#   TF_AI_PROVIDER=ollama        OHMAIL_OLLAMA_BASE_URL; requests go to the model server you name
# The OHMAIL_ names are on purpose: compose reads a variable from your shell before this file,
# and OPENAI_API_KEY, OLLAMA_BASE_URL and their like are what other tools on your machine read.
# Inside the containers they carry the plain names. With another provider than Anthropic there
# are no workflow proposals, as in the desktop app. docs/self-host/README.md has the details.
#TF_AI_PROVIDER=
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#OHMAIL_OPENAI_API_KEY=
#OHMAIL_OPENCODE_GO_API_KEY=

# Where your Ollama answers, as the api and the organizer reach it: a service on this stack's
# network (http://ollama:11434 for one you add to the compose file) or an IP address. There is
# no default: inside a container 127.0.0.1 is the container itself. `localhost` and names under
# .local or .internal are refused. A private address or plain http needs TF_AI_ALLOW_PRIVATE=1.
# A model tag Ollama forwards itself (its :cloud models) goes on to ollama.com.
#OHMAIL_OLLAMA_BASE_URL=

# The models, when you want others than the defaults: one answers suggestions, the other
# writes drafts. Unset: Anthropic's own, gpt-4.1-mini and gpt-4.1 on OpenAI, llama3.2 on
# Ollama. OpenCode Go has no default and needs both, from the model ids this app can drive —
# the api names them if a value is not one. Free and preview models are yours to choose; their
# upstream data terms can differ from the paid models', and ohmail cannot check them.
#TF_AI_CLASSIFY_MODEL=
#TF_AI_DRAFT_MODEL=

# Set to exactly `1` to let OHMAIL_OLLAMA_BASE_URL or OHMAIL_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL use plain http
# or an address written as a private or loopback IP. Off, a model address must be https, and
# such an address is refused. Names are not looked up, so a name on your own network passes
# either way: the address you write is what decides where your mail goes.
#TF_AI_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# A gateway in front of Anthropic, if you use one. Unset is api.anthropic.com.
#OHMAIL_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# How long one model call may take, in milliseconds. Unset: 10 s for a suggestion and 25 s
# for a draft on the api, 30 s on the organizer. A slow local model holds the organizer's mail
# cycle while it answers, so raise this with that in mind.
#TF_AI_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Set to exactly `1` when your mail server lives on your own LAN (a private IP or an
# internal hostname). Private-network targets are refused by default, because on a
# multi-user server this dials whatever address a signed-in user typed — and the refusal
# applies both to the add-mailbox probe and to every sync the organizer makes afterwards.
# It has to be set on BOTH services or neither: the api checks the server when you add the
# mailbox and the organizer checks it again every time it connects, so a value on only one
# of them is a mailbox that connects when you add it and then never syncs.
#TF_PROBE_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# Set to exactly `1` when your UnifiedPush DISTRIBUTOR lives on your own LAN (an `ntfy`
# beside this server). The mobile app can register an endpoint so your server wakes the
# phone when mail arrives — a signal with no subject, no sender and no count in it. By
# default that endpoint must be `https` and must resolve to a public address, because the
# organizer POSTs to it unattended for as long as the registration lives.
#
# A SEPARATE switch from TF_PROBE_ALLOW_PRIVATE on purpose. The two answer different
# questions: "may this install reach the mail server whose address I entered" and "may it
# POST to an endpoint a phone registered, for as long as that registration lives". Saying
# yes to the first is not saying yes to the second.
#
# It has to be set on BOTH services or neither — the api validates the registration and
# the organizer dials it, and a value on only one of them is a phone with a registration
# nothing ever rings.
#TF_PUSH_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# ── Encrypted new-mail wakes: this install's own VAPID keypair ────────────────────────────
#
# The mobile app registers with your phone's UnifiedPush distributor by handing it a PUBLIC
# key, and from then on it renders only wakes signed with the matching PRIVATE key. So a wake
# a phone will actually act on needs a keypair, and it has to be YOURS — never copied from
# another install, ours included. Two installs sharing a private key means either can send
# wakes the other's phones accept, and neither operator can tell.
#
# Generate one on this machine, nothing is sent anywhere:
#
#     node scripts/vapid-keygen.mjs
#
# and paste the two lines it prints below. `node scripts/vapid-keygen.mjs --check` verifies
# what is set — including whether the two halves are actually a pair, which is the mistake a
# copy-paste makes and the one nothing else will ever report: phones register happily and
# never ring.
#
# The public key goes to BOTH services (the api serves it to phones, the organizer puts it in
# every wake); the private key goes to the organizer alone, because the api never signs.
# Leave both empty and encrypted wakes are simply off — mail still arrives, the app still
# syncs when you open it, and a raw consumer (an `ntfy` topic you watch yourself) still gets
# the plain fifteen-byte wake. Set them WRONG and the organizer refuses to send any wake at
# all, deliberately, rather than let the arm that still works hide a broken configuration.
#
# Rotating the pair costs one re-registration per phone and nothing else.
#TF_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
#TF_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Optional: a mailto: or https: URI a push service can use to contact you about a misbehaving
# sender (RFC 8292's `sub`). Omitted from the signed token when empty, which is conformant.
#TF_VAPID_SUBJECT=

# Operational alerting. Both services check this install and page you: the organizer on its
# own (mailboxes, sends, storage), and the api, which is what notices a stopped organizer.
#
# TF_ALERT_SECRET (24+ characters, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 24`) arms the api's half: its alert
# endpoints (/internal/alerts, for your monitor) and its own alert pass, which runs inside the
# api every minute. Absent means the alert endpoints answer 404 and the api pages nobody; the
# organizer still pages on the channels below, and logs every alert either way, but nothing
# pages a stopped database (see below): set the secret together with the channels.
#
# Three channels, each optional. Set two: one vendor's outage should not silence the pager.
#  - TF_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL: alerts POSTed as JSON (ntfy.sh, Slack/Discord hooks, PagerDuty).
#    Both services page here.
#  - TF_ALERT_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and TF_ALERT_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID: a bot from BotFather and the
#    chat it posts to (a numeric id or @channelname). Set both. Both services page here.
#  - TF_ALERT_EMAIL: one address the api mails alerts to, through SMTP_URL and MAIL_FROM above
#    (point them at a real relay: the bundled mailpit only keeps mail on this box). Only the
#    api sends these mails.
# With the secret set, the api's /health lists the channels its pass delivers to under
# `alertSinks` (an empty list means it pages nobody).
# The two services watch each other's channels: when every channel of one keeps refusing, the
# other pages alert_driver_undeliverable (one dead channel beside a working one is
# alert_sink_degraded instead). A service with no channel at all, or none that could be built,
# is a warning signal on /internal/alerts, not a page (TF_ALERT_EMAIL alone leaves the
# organizer with none; the api carries its findings until a webhook or the Telegram pair is
# set). When every channel of both is down nothing can page: the only signals left are
# `"pager": "dead"` on both /health endpoints and an alerts_undeliverable log line once an
# hour. Two channels on one vendor share its outage: use two vendors.
#
# A database the alert passes cannot use pages alert_db_unreadable, sent from memory: one that is
# stopped or unreachable, one that refuses their work (read-only, out of disk, memory or
# connections, shut down), and one too slow for a pass's reads to finish within 30 s. A stopped
# database is paged by the api's alert pass, so only with TF_ALERT_SECRET set and while the api
# stays up: alert_db_unreadable:api on its channels, on the second failed minute, then at most
# once an hour (mail every 6 h), and one resolved notice to the webhook and Telegram when the
# database is back. The organizer holds its leader lock on the database, so it stops with a
# stopped one and runs no pass until it is back; it pages alert_db_unreadable:worker only while
# its lock session stays open (a hung, read-only or full database, too many connections).
# TF_ALERT_HEARTBEAT_URL below catches a dead database without the secret, and a dead box.
#TF_ALERT_SECRET=
#TF_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
#TF_ALERT_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
#TF_ALERT_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
#TF_ALERT_EMAIL=

# The dead-man heartbeat, optional: the way to catch a dead database or a dead box, which no
# page from inside can report. Create a check at an outside service (Healthchecks.io, Better
# Stack, Cronitor; suggested period 2 minutes, grace 5) and put its ping URL here. The
# organizer requests it, a plain GET with nothing in it, after every alert pass it completes,
# and so does the api when TF_ALERT_SECRET is set; with the same URL on both, the check alarms
# only when neither completes a pass. Unset means off. A value that is not one plain http(s)
# URL logs alert_heartbeat_unusable and sends nothing.
#TF_ALERT_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# The repair of empty messages an older version could store in a letter's place: the organizer
# imports the letter, and moves one it had filed into the ohmail Screener folder back to your
# Inbox. Unset (or `apply`) repairs. `dry` reads each affected message once per organizer start,
# logs `blank_repair` lines counting what the repair would do, and changes nothing; `off` does
# nothing. Any other value stops the organizer at start, naming this variable.
#OHMAIL_BLANK_REPAIR=

# Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online mailboxes need an app registration; password/IMAP
# mailboxes need none of this. It matters more than it used to: Microsoft has been
# switching basic authentication off, and on a tenant where that has already happened an
# app password will not connect at all.
#
# THERE ARE TWO WAYS IN. Set either block, or neither. If you set both, the app uses your
# own registration — it is yours, and it does not put your users behind a client id that
# other installs share.

# ── OPTION 1: BRING YOUR OWN REGISTRATION (a confidential app, with a secret) ──
#
# Register your own application in Entra, then set the four values below. Register
#   <OHMAIL_ORIGIN>/api/mailboxes/oauth/microsoft/callback
# as the redirect URI — the stack derives exactly that value by default, so only set
# MS_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI if your registration deliberately uses a different one.
# Delegated permissions: IMAP.AccessAsUser.All, SMTP.Send, openid, email (offline_access
# is requested automatically — without it a mailbox stops syncing after an hour).
#MS_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#MS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#MS_OAUTH_TENANT=
#MS_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=

# ── OPTION 2: THE DEVICE-CODE FLOW (a public app, no secret, no redirect URI) ──
#
# Connecting a mailbox this way shows the person a short code and a URL; they enter it at
# microsoft.com on any device, approve the sign-in, and Microsoft issues the access
# DIRECTLY TO THIS SERVER. Nothing about the exchange passes through ohmail's
# infrastructure, and there is no redirect URI — which is exactly why it works on a server
# whose hostname Microsoft has never heard of.
#
# MS_DEVICE_CLIENT_ID is the Application (client) ID of a PUBLIC application registration.
# It is a DIFFERENT registration from option 1 and the ids are not interchangeable: a
# confidential app's id fails this flow outright, because the flow sends no secret and
# Entra answers `unauthorized_client`. So this is its own variable and the app reads only
# this one for this door.
#
# You can point it at your own public registration today. If you do, in Entra:
#   · Platform "Mobile and desktop applications", and
#   · Authentication -> Advanced settings -> "Allow public client flows" = Yes. Without
#     this the flow returns `unauthorized_client` and nothing works. It is the single
#     easiest step to miss.
#   · Same delegated permissions as option 1. NO client secret — do not create one; this
#     door refuses to send one deliberately.
#
# ohmail's own shared public client id will be published here once it has been registered
# and tested against real Microsoft. Until that line exists, this variable works with a
# registration you make yourself.
#
# MS_DEVICE_TENANT is optional and defaults to `common`, which is what lets both a work
# mailbox and an outlook.com mailbox sign in. Set it only for a single-tenant registration.
#
# NOTE: set this on BOTH the `api` and the `organizer` services. The api runs the sign-in;
# the organizer renews the access afterwards, for ever, and a refresh token can only be
# renewed by the application that issued it. Armed on the api alone, a mailbox connects and
# then stops syncing about an hour later.
#MS_DEVICE_CLIENT_ID=
#MS_DEVICE_TENANT=

# ── Storage details (defaults are the bundled RustFS; change only for an external store) ──

# The staging store's endpoint, region, access key and bucket. To use an existing
# S3-compatible service instead of the bundled RustFS, set all of these to that service's
# values — and remember the organizer sweeps the same bucket the api mints into.
#S3_ENDPOINT=http://rustfs:9000
#S3_REGION=us-east-1
#RUSTFS_ROOT_USER=ohmail-staging
#S3_BUCKET=ohmail-staging

# Where a BROWSER uploads staged attachments. Leave this unset: uploads then go to
# <OHMAIL_ORIGIN>/<bucket>/…, and the proxy carries them to RustFS — nothing extra to expose.
# A different value only serves NON-BROWSER clients (the desktop app): the web app may only
# talk to its own origin, so an off-origin endpoint here means browser uploads are refused
# by the browser itself. If S3_ENDPOINT names an external store and you still want browser
# uploads, route /<bucket>/* to that store in the Caddyfile instead — it must accept requests
# addressed under this origin (your own RustFS does; Amazon's does not).
#S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=
